media: cedrus: H.264 encoder video node for the H616 video engine

Add a second video node (cedrus-enc) to the Cedrus driver: a stateful
V4L2 encoder taking NV12 and producing H.264 (CAVLC, Baseline/Main,
fixed QP, one reference frame, no B frames). It shares the decoder's
v4l2_m2m_dev, so encode and decode jobs serialise on the one engine and
the IRQ handler and watchdog are common; the decoder is unchanged.

The encode sequence follows Bootlin's Cedrus H.264 encoder for the
V3s/S3 (the driver writes SPS, PPS and slice header through the
encoder's put-bits port, then starts the encoder). The H616 video engine
differs from the V3s in four ways that all matter for a correct stream:
- the encoder-block address registers take the bus address >> 8;
- the ISP input addresses stay plain bus addresses;
- VE_ISP_SCALER_SIZE sets the encoder's macroblock grid and counts in
  half macroblocks;
- the macroblock-info, sub-pixel and reconstruction buffers must start
  zeroed, as the encoder reads neighbour context back from them.

The H616 compatible gets a variant with the H6 decoder set (unchanged
decoder behaviour and clock) plus CEDRUS_CAPABILITY_H264_ENC. The codec
ops gain an optional finish() hook, called before buffer completion, so
the encoder can set the coded payload size and keyframe flag.

Validated on an Orange Pi Zero 2W (H618), kernel 6.18.35: ffmpeg
h264_v4l2m2m encodes 1920x1088 NV12 at about 6.5 ms per frame; streams
decode without errors (IDR + P, GOP from the client); HEVC decode on the
same engine runs concurrently.

